{"name": "n2_rebirth_bj", "sha256": "445732eacdb1f6388a81eb4955363fe5d7f388d081feee1286e4c66202f1760f", "words": 112, "headline": "Human Written", "prediction_short": "Human", "fraction_ai": 4.1787640845563306e-22, "fraction_ai_assisted": 0.0, "fraction_human": 1.0, "version": "4.1", "source": "new", "dashboard_link": "https://www.pangram.com/history/d09ef29a-c0be-4cd6-a42f-99383aa60007", "utc": "2026-10-23T13:00:52Z", "windows": [["Human Written", "High", 120, 0, 699, "Stream entry’s greatest prize is a “release date”: AN 3.88 allows at most seven more lives before final awakening. There is actually quite a lot of modern academic evidence for rebirth. But who’s going around getting reborn if no permanent self exists? Buddhists don’t imagine any permanent self-essence/soul moving between bodies. Rather, rebirth is seen as a causal process: there is a continuity (often called a “mind-stream”), but there is not a “thing” that’s moving from one life to the next. Craving and clinging lead to becoming, and becoming leads to birth. More like a driverless car. The later Buddhist philosopher Milindapañha compared rebirth to one lamp being used to lighting another."]]}
